<body>
<div class="shell">
<div class="masthead"><div class="brand"><span class="mark" aria-hidden="true"></span>Boundary Conditions</div><div class="edition">Field notes on software / No. 007</div></div>
<main>
<header class="hero">
<div class="eyebrow">Engineering / Distributed systems</div>
<h1>Retries Aren'"'t Idempotent<br><em>By Accident.</em></h1>
<p class=\"dek\">A timeout tells you the answer is missing.<br>It tells you nothing about whether the work happened.</p>
<div class=\"meta\"><span>Systems notebook</span><span>6 September 2026</span><span>5 min read</span></div>
</header>
<div class=\"article-layout\">
<aside aria-label=\"Article theme\"><div class=\"side-label\">The central distinction</div><div class=\"side-note\">Repeat the request.<br>Preserve the effect.</div><div class=\"side-rule\"></div><p class=\"side-small\">Reliability is a delivery problem.<br>Idempotency is a state problem.</p></aside>
<article>
<p class=\"intro\">Your client submits a payment. The server commits it, but the connection drops before the response arrives. The client sees a timeout and retries. Without an explicit agreement about what makes those two requests the same operation, you have just bought reliability at the price of a second charge.</p>
<p>Backoff and jitter help a struggling service recover. They cannot tell it whether a request is new work or an uncertain repeat. Idempotency means repeating an operation preserves its intended effect. For a state-changing API, that property needs a protocol and durable state, not merely a retry loop.</p>
<section aria-labelledby=\"identity\">
<h2 id=\"identity\"><span class=\"section-num\">01 /</span> Give the intent an identity</h2>
<p>Generate an idempotency key when the user creates a logical operation, before the first network attempt. Persist it alongside the pending operation and reuse it for every retry, including after a client restart. Generating a fresh UUID inside the retry loop gives every duplicate a perfectly unique identity.</p>
<p>Scope the key to an authenticated tenant and operation type. Store a fingerprint of the validated, normalized request too. The same key with different payment details should produce a conflict, not silently return an unrelated receipt. Conversely, identical payloads with different keys may represent two legitimate purchases: payload equality is not intent.</p>
<figure>
<div class=\"figure-label\">Fig. 01 / One intent, two deliveries</div>
<svg class=\"flow\" viewBox=\"0 0 650 266\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"flow-title flow-desc\">
<title id=\"flow-title\">A retry reuses the original idempotency key</title>
<desc id=\"flow-desc\">The client sends POST with key pay_7f. The server commits the payment and receipt, but the response is lost. The client retries with the same key and receives the stored receipt without a second payment.</desc>
<defs><marker id=\"arrow\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"6\" refY=\"3.5\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0 0L7 3.5L0 7Z\" fill=\"#275d45\"/></marker></defs>
