</svg>
<figcaption>A lost response creates uncertainty at the client, not permission to repeat the side effect.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section aria-labelledby=\"atomic\">
<h2 id=\"atomic\"><span class=\"section-num\">02 /</span> Make the decision atomic</h2>
<p>A naive “look up the key, then process” handler races. Two workers can both observe an absent key and both execute. Use a database uniqueness constraint to arbitrate ownership, and put the business mutation and recorded response in the same transaction. Otherwise, a crash between them reopens the duplicate window.</p>
<div class=\"code-box\"><div class=\"code-label\"><span>POST /payments</span><b>Python-like pseudocode</b></div><pre><code><span class=\"comment\"># UNIQUE(tenant_id, operation, key) in the database</span>
scope = (auth.tenant_id, <span class=\"string\">\"create_payment\"</span>, request.key)
fingerprint = hash_canonical(validated_payment)

<span class=\"keyword\">with</span> db.transaction():
    <span class=\"comment\"># INSERT ON CONFLICT; then SELECT FOR UPDATE.</span>
    <span class=\"comment\"># Competing inserts wait for commit or rollback.</span>
    record = claim_and_lock(scope, fingerprint)
    <span class=\"keyword\">if</span> record.fingerprint "'!= fingerprint:
        <span class="keyword">raise</span> Conflict(<span class="string">"Key reused with different input"</span>)
    <span class="keyword">if</span> record.response <span class="keyword">is not</span> None:
        response = record.response
    <span class="keyword">else</span>:
        payment = create_payment_in_this_database()
        response = Response(201, payment.receipt())
        record.save_response(response)

<span class="keyword">return</span> response <span class="comment"># Only after the transaction commits.</span></code></pre></div>
<p>Here, an uncommitted claim disappears if the transaction rolls back; a committed claim includes the receipt. A concurrent request waits, then reads the winner’s result. Set a bounded wait and return a documented retryable response if it expires. Store the status and response body needed for replay, rather than rebuilding a receipt from business data that may have changed.</p>
</section>
<section aria-labelledby="boundary">
<h2 id="boundary"><span class="section-num">03 /</span> Know where the guarantee ends</h2>
<p>The example only protects effects inside that database. A card processor, email service, or message broker does not participate in its transaction. Calling one while holding the lock cannot make its effect roll back. Use the downstream service’s idempotency facility with a stable derived key, or write an outbox entry atomically and let a worker deliver it. An outbox still needs duplicate-safe consumers.</p>
<p>Retention is also part of the contract. If keys expire after 24 hours, a retry on day two may execute again. Choose a retention window that covers realistic retries, document it, and consider a permanent business identifier for operations that must never recur. Idempotency records are correctness state; evicting them is a semantic decision.</p>
<p>Finally, distinguish rejected input from an unknown outcome. Validation failures can be corrected before an operation is claimed. A timeout after a downstream call demands reconciliation or a retry with the same identity. Test that boundary deliberately: drop responses after commit, race duplicate requests, and crash workers between steps.</p>
</section>
<div class="takeaway"><div class="eyebrow">The takeaway</div><p>A retry asks for another answer. A well-designed idempotency key ensures it does not accidentally ask for another effect.</p></div>
</article>
